[38;5;231m⏺[39m That answer changes the classification, so I'm stopping to say so.

  [1mUpgrading: bounded → architectural.[0m Persisting the userId names storage this repo doesn't have — there's no storage
  layer, no session concept, no module that owns client-side state. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a single script with two functions and an
  event listener. The first edit still looks like one line ([38;5;153msessionStorage.setItem(...)[39m), but the outcome you named is a
  new subsystem, and the brainstorming ratchet is one-way: I step up rather than talk myself into calling it small. So
  this gets the full treatment — approaches, a sectioned design, and a written spec before any code.

  One thing I want on the table before the questions, because it's a consequence beyond the lines I'd touch: persisting
  identity in browser storage makes it readable by any script on the origin, so it's XSS-exposed, and [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m
  survives browser restarts on shared machines. It's a normal thing to do for a non-authoritative display value ("logged
  in as…"); it is not safe as something the app later [3mtrusts[0m to decide who someone is. That distinction drives most of
  the design, so it's the first thing I need to pin down.
